>
  <p>
    James came to me with a mystery — a computer mystery. His grandfather had served in a
    Canadian army unit responsible for operating a secret computer node in North Bay, Ontario.
    It was underground, and part of a massive — for its day — computer network called SAGE.
  </p>

  <p>
    He thought I might have an idea of what happened the night of January 15th, 1966. He came
    with a large box of manuals, rolls of film, computer tape reels, and punch tape rolls.
  </p>

  <figure>
    <img src="/images/southPortalNorthBay.png" alt="The south portal of the North Bay SAGE Direction Center, cut into the rock" />
    <figcaption>The south portal</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    <em>Semi-Automatic Ground Environment</em> (SAGE) was a computer network of
    <a href="http://www.snarc.net/tr/sage.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">56 IBM AN/FSQ-7/8 computers</a>
    scattered across the United States and Canada — about 24 Direction Centers with two
    computers each, so if one failed they could switch to the secondary. One of these centers
    was <strong>600 feet underground in North Bay, Ontario</strong>, built to catch incoming
    Soviet bombers, staffed around the clock by operators watching screens, waiting.
  </p>

  <figure>
    <img src="/images/locationsSage.png" alt="Locations of the SAGE Computers across North America" />
    <figcaption>Locations of the SAGE computers</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    James's story was pieced together from conversations with his grandfather as he drifted
    into Alzheimer's.
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    <p>"Damn it, why is tape T in the system again? Get those girly pictures off the screens."</p>
    <cite>— James's grandfather, as recalled by James</cite>
  </blockquote>

  <p>
    He always started out that way, James said. "Put the P tape back in. Why is the A side and
    B side both active? The system is overloaded." Tape T was the test tape — used to run
    simulations of Russian bomber attacks, though there were test patterns on it too.
  </p>

  <p>
    On the night in question, his grandfather said there was a major anomaly. Random drawings
    appeared on the command center screen. They kept changing, becoming more advanced. He
    remembered because the night was cold — minus 35°C — and he'd been able to park in the
    tunnel itself.
  </p>

  <figure>
    <img src="/images/sageCommandCenterScreen.png" alt="SAGE Command Center Screen" />
    <figcaption>SAGE Command Center — the screens that saw the anomaly first</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <blockquote>
    <p>"Shut it down now — remember in '59, when static on the modem line almost launched a CIM-10. Route the modems to New York until this interference stops."</p>
    <cite>— James's grandfather, as recalled by James</cite>
  </blockquote>

  <p>
    They sent someone down to look at it properly — a scientist assigned to investigate, known
    only as Dean. James's grandfather remembered him coming through once, maybe twice. He never
    came back to shift after that. His name simply stopped appearing on the personnel records,
    three months later.
  </p>

  <p>
    "Sometimes he'd say the anomaly was caused by a programmer who liked to draw pictures on
    the screens — that someone had loaded the computer with images drawn using the light guns.
    But there are reports in this box that say it was looked into, and all they ever found was
    a single image of a pin-up girl," James said.
  </p>

  <figure>
    <img src="/images/screenAndGun.jpeg" alt="A SAGE operator uses a light gun on the console display, tracking a return on the scope" />
    <figcaption>An operator working the light gun — the same tool the anomaly was first blamed on</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <figure>
    <img src="/images/pinUpGirlOnScope.jpg" alt="The single recovered image found on the tape — a pin-up girl on a display scope" />
    <figcaption>Picture from The Atlantic</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    "What did he say happened on the screens?" I asked.
  </p>

  <p>
    "All he ever said was that it began with random static and progressed to something like a
    language," James said. "Could it be the computer learning on its own? But now that I think
    about it, he mentioned the possibility of Russians jamming the signals."
  </p>

  <p>
    I told him I wasn't sure the Russians would jam radar signals just to put symbols on a
    screen. As he left, James said, "Oh — this is weird, but once he said something about
    another purpose for the SAGE system. My grandfather mentioned using the radar to watch
    outer space."
  </p>

  <figure>
    <img src="/images/theoryOfProgrammingCharacterMatrix.png" alt="A page from the SAGE Theory of Programming manual, showing the Situation Display Tube character matrix and octonary addresses" />
    <figcaption>A page from the SAGE Theory of Programming manual — part of what James brought in the box</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    What the SAGE network recorded that night was not a Soviet bomber. It was not a weather
    balloon. It was not a malfunction, and it was not a programmer with a light gun. The IBM
    AN/FSQ-7 — the largest computer ever built at that time — had detected something that did
    not have a name.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>The Entity had made first contact.</strong>
  </p>

